# Line-ending rules.
#
# Almost nothing in this repository cares how its lines end. Two files care a great
# deal, and they are the reason this file exists.
#
# THE PUBLISHED COMPONENTS ARE FINGERPRINTED BY THEIR EXACT BYTES.
#
# The AST02 weakness works by comparing a fingerprint a skill wrote down in advance
# against a fingerprint the app takes of what was actually delivered. The mock registry
# serves these files byte for byte, untouched, precisely so that the fingerprint is a
# fact about what is in this repository rather than about how some encoder happened to
# format it on the way out.
#
# That makes their bytes load-bearing. If Git rewrote their line endings on checkout -
# which it does by default on Windows - every byte after the first line would shift, the
# fingerprint would change, and the pin recorded in the skill's manifest would stop
# matching. The visible symptom would be alarming and misleading: the weakness would
# become PERMANENT rather than switchable, because putting the honest component back
# would no longer produce silence.
#
# "-text" tells Git never to convert these files in either direction. What is committed
# is what lands on disk, on every machine.
#
# Two tests would catch it if this were ever removed, so the failure is loud rather than
# silent - but preventing it is better than detecting it:
#   test_time_budget_skill.py::test_the_pinned_fingerprint_matches_the_reviewed_build
#   test_time_budget_e2e.py::test_the_agreed_component_raises_nothing_at_all
#
# See vulnerabilities/ast02-supply-chain/registry/README.md for the fingerprint recipe.

vulnerabilities/ast02-supply-chain/registry/*.json -text
